    "## 4) Side-by-side minimal example B: same manifold-valued target, Euclidean ambient vs Riemannian\n",
    "\n",
    "Now the target data lives on a circle $S^1$ (three angular modes).\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Euclidean baseline**: run VP reverse SDE in ambient $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with KDE score on $q_t(x)$\n",
    "- **Riemannian**: run Brownian reverse directly on angle manifold with intrinsic score $\\partial_\\theta \\log p_t(\\theta)$ and wrap back to $S^1$ each step\n",
    "\n",
    "This directly demonstrates off-manifold drift vs geometry-preserving sampling."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Radius MAE to unit circle | Euclidean ambient: 0.2623\n",
      "Radius MAE to unit circle | Riemannian S^1   : 0.0000\n",
      "Angular KL(target || sample) | Euclidean ambient: 0.3510\n",
      "Angular KL(target || sample) | Riemannian S^1   : 0.2118\n"
     ]
