    "## 5) Why Euclidean fails here and RMSDE succeeds\n",
    "\n",
    "In the circle experiment, the true state space is $S^1$.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Euclidean reverse diffusion evolves in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Even with good angular score hints, noise/drift acts in radial directions too, so samples leave the manifold. This is off-manifold drift.\n",
    "- Riemannian reverse diffusion evolves intrinsically (angle tangent) and maps back to the manifold every step (`exp`/wrapping), preserving constraints by construction.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the structural difference you were asking about: RMSDE is not just a different implementation; it changes the state space, differential operators, and valid denoising directions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6) Practical takeaways for your pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "Use Euclidean LDM-style diffusion when:\n",
    "- latent space is effectively unconstrained Euclidean,\n",
    "- geometry/topology of data manifold is not a primary source of error.\n",
    "\n",
    "Consider RMSDE when:\n",
    "- latents are constrained (unit norm, rotation groups, hyperbolic/spherical structure),\n",
    "- you observe off-manifold artifacts or geometry-inconsistent interpolation,\n",
    "- likelihood/sampling behavior should respect intrinsic distances and tangent fields.\n",
    "\n",
    "In short: RMSDE relaxes the implicit Euclidean assumption and replaces it with manifold-aware stochastic dynamics."
   ]
